 ## Thread and draft rules
 
-All mobile entry points resume the current conversation. Opening or closing the popup never
-creates a chat session, clears messages or changes an active send's target. An empty provider
-loads the server-current transcript through the existing current-chat path and cache.
-Typed questions and composer microphone transcripts continue that same conversation.
-The mobile popup has no Past chats or New Chat controls.
+Opening or closing the popup never creates a chat session, clears messages or changes an active
+send's target. An empty provider reloads the open chat: the discussion that was open before a
+restart (`lastChatSessionId`, owner-scoped) or, when there is none, the server-current transcript
+through the existing current-chat path and cache. Typed questions and composer microphone
+transcripts continue the open chat.
+
+The header has New chat and Past chats on the right, balanced by Close on the left so the dismissal
+handle stays centered. New chat only clears the local projection; the server session is created on
+the first send (`prepareChatSession`). Past chats (`widgets/chat_history_sheet.dart`) lists saved
+chats newest first by day, opens one explicitly by id, starts a new one, and deletes one after
+confirmation (long press). The clone's server moves a chat up the list when a message is added to
+it (`updated_at`), so the app never touches a session for ordering.
+
+Ask Omi from a conversation (`initialChatContext` of type `conversation`) never continues whatever
+chat is open: it resumes the chat last used about that conversation (`chatForConversation`,
+owner-scoped, learned from each send with that context and from the `page_context` the clone's
+server keeps on human messages) or starts a new one. A new chat left without a message is dropped
+when the popup closes, so the next opening resumes the open chat again.
+
+The single thread that existed before separate chats is moved server-side into a "Discussion
+d'origine" session by the clone's migration (`omi-local/migrer-chat.py`); the app shows it like any
+other saved chat.
 
 Existing server sessions and stored messages remain intact. Shared session APIs stay available
 for their other consumers; this change does not merge or delete previously saved sessions.
